{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[A]My old [G]hen is a [A]good old [G]hen
[A]She lays [G]eggs for the [E]railroad [A]men
[A]Sometimes [G]one, [A]sometimes [G]two
[A]Sometimes [G]enough for the [E]whole damn [A]crew
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[Am]Cluck old [G]hen, [Am]cluck and [G]sing
You [Am]ain't laid an [G]egg since [E]way last [Am]spring
[Am]Cluck old [G]hen, [Am]cackle and [G]squall
You [Am]ain't laid an [G]egg since [E]way last [Am]fall
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Cluck old hen, when I tell you
Cluck old hen, or I’m gonna sell you
Last time she cackled, she cackled in the lot,
Next time she cackled, she’ll cackle in the pot;
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Cluck ol hen, I’m tired of you
Gonna make me some chicken stew
Cluck ol hen, your luck’s all gone
Gonna cook you up and put some gravy on
{end_of_chorus}
